41. Real world testing surfaces a structural RAG limitation, not a bug

Streamlit app tested live with free form questions beyond the golden set.

Correct refusals: "allocation for education" and "allocation for defence" both returned "insufficient data" correctly, since no such figures actually appear in this speech. This is honest behavior, not a failure, matching the PROJECT_SCOPE requirement for an insufficient data fallback when information genuinely is not present.

Structural limitation found: counting questions, "how many times is Modi mentioned," cannot be answered by retrieval based RAG at all, regardless of chunk size or TOP_K tuning. Retrieval only surfaces the 5 most semantically similar chunks out of roughly 130, and a counting question has no meaningful semantic anchor, it needs visibility into the entire document, not the most relevant fragments of it. This maps to the query classification step already described in PROJECT_SCOPE section 7.3, factual and comparative queries suit retrieval, counting and exhaustive queries do not and need a different mechanism entirely, for example direct text search or structured extraction, not chunk retrieval.

Decision: log this as a known category limitation of the current architecture, not a defect to fix within this bounded sandbox. Any fix would require a query classification layer routing counting-type questions away from RAG entirely, which is a larger architectural addition than this experiment's scope covers.

