58. Year-filtering gap identified during cross-year check

Ran the planned cross-year check in the deployed Streamlit app, asked two year-specific questions with no year in the retrieved-chunk metadata matching the asked year, "education budget in year 2021" retrieved chunks from 1998-99, 1955-56, 1993-94, 1954-55, and 1991-92, none within a decade of the asked year. Same pattern on "defence budget in 2020." System correctly returned "insufficient data" rather than fabricate an answer from the wrong-year chunks, so the safety fallback held.

Root cause: retrieval is pure   semantic similarity with no year-based filtering, and specific years like "2020" or "2021" are weak lexical signals against strong, frequent topic words like "education" or "defence" that recur across decades of speeches. This is a distinct failure mode from the previously logged inter-context misattribution, that one blends numbers across correctly retrieved years, this one fails to retrieve the correct year at all.

Decision: logging as a known limitation, not fixing today. Scoped fix already identified, ChromaDB metadata filtering on the year field during query, using the already-populated year metadata on every chunk. Consistent with the existing "on the horizon" plan for query routing via metadata filtering, not a new architecture decision, just a concrete trigger for one already planned.

